proofs.proof_tex:
The ridge argument in thm:adjoint-covariance proves finite-sample positive definiteness and hence existence of every displayed inverse.  Conditional on the data, the Gaussian multipliers make \(Z^\star\) exactly centered Gaussian with covariance
\[
 \operatorname{Cov}^\star(Z^\star)
 =\frac1{L_n}\sum_g\frac n{n_g}\frac1{n_g}
 \sum_i(\widehat\Psi_{g,h_n}^{(-r(i))}(X_{g,i})-\overline\Psi_{g,r(i)})
 (\widehat\Psi_{g,h_n}^{(-r(i))}(X_{g,i})-\overline\Psi_{g,r(i)})^{\mathsf T}
 =\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj}}.
\tag{50}
\]
Thus thm:adjoint-covariance implies conditional weak convergence, uniformly in \(P\), to \(N(0,V(P))\).  The limiting covariance is block diagonal by lem:full-critical-covariance.  Consequently, if \(Z\sim N(0,V(P))\), the variables \(Z_j^{\mathsf T}V_j(P)^{-1}Z_j\) are independent \(\chi^2_2\), and
\[
 \mathbb P\left\{\max_{1\leq j\leq J}
 Z_j^{\mathsf T}V_j(P)^{-1}Z_j\leq x\right\}
 =(1-e^{-x/2})^J,qquad x\geq0.
\tag{51}
\]
This continuous distribution is parameter free and its \((1-\gamma)\)-quantile is
\[
 c_{1-\gamma}=-2\log\{1-(1-\gamma)^{1/J}\}>0.
\tag{52}
\]
The inverse consistency in thm:adjoint-covariance and (50)--(52) imply
\(\widehat c_{1-\gamma}\to c_{1-\gamma}\) in probability, uniformly over the class.  Applying the same continuous quadratic maximum to the joint limit in thm:joint-critical-clt yields
\[
 \inf_{P\in\mathcal F_s}\mathbb P_P\left\{
 \max_j\frac n{L_n}(\widehat\tau_j-\tau_j(P))^{\mathsf T}
 (\widehat V_{\mathrm{adj},jj}^R)^{-1}
 (\widehat\tau_j-\tau_j(P))\leq\widehat c_{1-\gamma}
 \right\}\longrightarrow1-\gamma.
\]
The event in braces is exactly \(\{\tau_j(P)\in\mathcal E_j\text{ for every }j\}\), proving the assertion.  The critical value is well defined even in exceptional finite samples because it is a quantile of a nonnegative random variable and the ridge makes every inverse total.
